<p>If you’re looking for a <strong>coding platform for kids</strong> that goes beyond moving a character around the screen and also helps you with math, logic, science and digital citizenship, Codexia is worth a look. It’s designed for students aged 4 to 12, combines blocks with real code, reads instructions aloud for children who can’t read yet, and includes a dashboard so the teacher can assign work and track progress. Here I’ll explain what it is, what it includes and, above all, share ten concrete ideas for bringing it into your classroom.</p>

<h2>What is Codexia?</h2>
<p>Codexia is a gamified educational platform developed by Grupo Logic that teaches coding, math, logic, artificial intelligence and other subjects to children aged 4 to 12.</p>
<p>It works like a video game. Each subject is a region with worlds that unlock as the student progresses. The child picks a world, builds a program with blocks or writes code, runs it and watches the hero solve the challenge by following those instructions. At the end, they earn stars, coins, achievements and items for their avatar.</p>
<p>What makes it valuable for a school is that it isn’t a standalone game but a curriculum organized into ten subjects, with activities that grow in difficulty. It runs on computers and is optimized for school tablets.</p>

<h2>Why teach computational thinking from an early age?</h2>
<p>Computational thinking isn’t “learning to use the computer.” It’s breaking a problem down into steps, recognizing patterns, putting instructions in order and checking what went wrong when something doesn’t work. These are skills you already work on when a child solves a math problem in stages or puts the parts of a story in order.</p>
<p>Coding at an early age makes that process visible: if the robot didn’t reach the star, you have to check the sequence. That culture of trying, failing and fixing is useful in any subject. The hard part for teachers is usually having a structured path that is age-appropriate and doesn’t require the child to already know how to read.</p>

