<h2>10 ideas for using Codexia in the classroom</h2>
<p>These ideas build on what the platform offers and on classroom routines you already know.</p>

<h3>1. A coding corner in kindergarten (ages 5 to 6)</h3>
<p>Set up rotating learning centers: building, picture stories, pretend play, and one with two or three tablets and headphones for the Preschool section. Since the voice narrates every instruction, children don’t depend on you to read. Assign math or early literacy activities from the dashboard and keep each rotation to 10 to 15 minutes. At the end, ask each child to tell you what the game asked them to do and how they solved it: you’re also working on speaking skills.</p>

<h3>2. Design your own level (grades 1 to 3, art and math)</h3>
<p>After playing several challenges, each pair draws their own level on grid paper: a hero, obstacles and a star as the goal. In art class they take care of color, characters and composition. Then they swap drawings and the other team writes, with arrows or words, the program that solves the level, counting steps and turns. That’s spatial awareness, left-right orientation and counting, with no screen involved.</p>

<h3>3. Pair programming for loops (grades 3 and 4)</h3>
<p>Work with two roles: the “pilot” operates the device and the “co-pilot” reads the challenge, proposes the strategy and checks the work. They switch every two challenges. When you get to loops, ask the co-pilot to identify the repeated steps before dragging a single block: “if we move forward three times in a row, how do we say that with <em>repetir(3)</em>?” Along the way they practice listening, reasoning and taking turns to speak.</p>

<h3>4. Geometry with challenges and notebooks (grades 4 and 5)</h3>
<p>Assign the Geometry worlds as a complement to your unit on perimeter, area or angles. The sequence: a short explanation on the board, 20 minutes of challenges and, at the end, each student picks one challenge and explains it in their notebook with a drawing and the operation they used. That way the platform isn’t reduced to “beating levels,” and you get written evidence of learning.</p>

<h3>5. The programmer’s journal (grades 2 to 5, language arts)</h3>
<p>At the end of each session, children write three sentences about a challenge: what they had to achieve, which instructions they used and what they fixed. Require sequence words such as first, next, then and finally. An algorithm is, after all, an instructional text.</p>

<h3>6. Basic physics with a real experiment (grades 3 to 5, science)</h3>
<p>Codexia includes worlds about forces, gravity, light, sound, heat and magnets. Use them as a bridge to hands-on experience: if you’re studying magnetism, start with magnets, paper clips and classroom objects so the children can form hypotheses; then move on to the challenges and wrap up by comparing what they observed in both moments. The screen doesn’t replace the experiment, but it reinforces the concepts.</p>

<h3>7. A digital citizenship assembly (grades 4 to 6)</h3>
<p>The Internet safety subject covers passwords, <em>phishing</em>, privacy and digital footprint. Split the class into teams, give each one a topic and ask them for a mini-presentation after completing the activities: “how to recognize a suspicious message” or “what leaves a digital footprint.” Close with a role-play where the class identifies the warning signs of a deceptive message.</p>

<h3>8. A first encounter with artificial intelligence (grades 5 and 6)</h3>
<p>The artificial intelligence module covers data, patterns and the first artificial neuron. Before starting it, do an unplugged activity: hand out cards with drawings of fruit and ask the class to invent rules to sort them by color, size or shape. After the module, discuss: what happens if the examples a machine receives are incomplete? In the <a href="/ia-para-docentes/">AI for teachers</a> section you’ll find more material to keep exploring the topic.</p>

<h3>9. A weekly challenge with fair recognition (all of elementary school)</h3>
<p>Every Monday, assign a “challenge of the week” to the whole class from the dashboard. On Friday, review stars and streaks, but recognition isn’t just for whoever scored the most points: it’s also for whoever best explained their strategy or helped others the most. Use achievements, coins and the avatar as motivation, without turning the classroom into a leaderboard that discourages those who go more slowly.</p>

<h3>10. Formative assessment with live statistics (all ages)</h3>
<p>During class, the dashboard shows you who is moving ahead and who is stuck on a challenge. Use it for flexible grouping: those who have mastered the topic move on to harder challenges or help out as peer tutors; those who are stuck work with you on a mini-explanation. Since you can assign by student or by class, you can differentiate without preparing three different worksheets. An exit ticket (“What did you learn? What was hard for you?”) fills in what the numbers don’t tell you.</p>

<h2>How to get started, step by step</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Get to know the platform yourself first.</strong> Codexia offers a 24-hour trial with access to all ten subjects; use it to explore the worlds you’re interested in.</li>
